.5 billion over the comparable prior year quarter.\n435:Estate and gift taxes—Net estate and gift tax receipts were $6.6 billion for the third quarter of fiscal year 2011. These\n437:receipts represent an increase of $0.9 billion over the same quarter in fiscal year 2010.\n439:Customs duties—Customs duties net of refunds were $7.1 billion for the third quarter of fiscal year 2011. This is an increase of $1.0 billion over the comparable prior year quarter.\n441:Miscellaneous receipts—Net miscellaneous receipts for the third quarter of fiscal year 2011 were $27.2 billion, an increase of $5.8 billion over the comparable prior year quarter. This change is due in part to deposits of earnings by Federal Reserve banks increasing by $6.0 billion.\n467:[In billions of dollars. Source: \"Monthly Treasury Statement of Receipts and Outlays of the United States Government\"]\n490:CHART FFO-A.— Monthly Receipts and Outlays, 2010-2011 (In billions of dollars)\n492:CHART FFO-B.— Budget Receipts by Source, Fiscal Year to Date, 2010-2011 (In billions of dollars)\n875:TABLE UST-1.—Elements of Change in Federal Reserve and Tax and Loan Note Account Balances 1\n879:| Fiscal year or month > Fiscal year or month | Federal Reserve accounts > Received directly (1) | Federal Reserve accounts > Received through remittance option tax and loan depositaries (2) | Credits and withdrawals > Credits and withdrawals | Tax and loan note accounts > Taxes 4 (4) | Tax and loan note accounts > Withdrawals (transfers to Federal Reserve accounts) (5) |\n908:TABLE UST-1.—Elements of Change in Federal Reserve and Tax and Loan Note Account Balances, con.\n912:| Fiscal year or month > Fiscal year or month | End of period > Federal Reserve (6) | End of period > SFP (7) | End of period > Tax and loan note accounts (8) | High > Federal Reserve (9) | High > SFP (10) | High > Tax and loan note accounts (11) | During period > Federal Reserve (12) | During period > SFP (13) | During period > Tax and loan note account